Yes. In an execution environment like this one, the model can often retrieve publicly accessible full-text files, save them, build a structured catalogue, and package the collection into a ZIP archive. That capability can vary by site and by the tools available to the future chat, so the instruction now explicitly requires a capability check and a fallback path.

I updated the executable document so the preferred deliverables are:

- a **ZIP research archive** containing acquired articles;
- an **article information table** covering every accepted source;
- a **topic-by-topic synthesis** relating the literature to the project's mechanisms of interest;
- an acquisition manifest/README;
- explicit accounting of downloaded, inaccessible, duplicate, rejected, and unevaluated sources.

When direct acquisition is unavailable, the model must instead provide the full article table plus **source-by-source lawful acquisition instructions**, including DOI/stable identifier, repository or publisher, public full-text location where known, and navigation steps.

It also now prohibits claiming an article was acquired unless the actual file was successfully obtained and verified.

